      "id": "white",
      "name": "Cleave",
      "description": "White and coat take about half the horse each - between 45 and 55 per cent, never outside 35 to 65. White has the head, the front and underside of the neck, the chest, the forelegs and the front of the shoulder; the coat keeps the withers, back, croup, hindquarters and hind legs. What makes it Cleave is the boundary: each field drives long narrow triangles into the other, straight along the sides and never convex, with smaller teeth splitting off the flanks of the big ones and the pattern of them different on the two sides. Detached spears of the same shape float within a hand's span of the line. Every edge is hard - no gradient, no roaning, no feathering anywhere on the horse. It takes two copies.",
